Yes. Animals give us a giant library of behavioral operators, especially if we separate literal function from the form that looks fun to us.

You can mine things like:

play fighting
→ skill rehearsal + social calibration

chase games
→ speed + prediction + turn-taking

courtship dances
→ signaling + timing + reciprocity + endurance

bower building
→ environmental display + aesthetic signaling

birdsong
→ identity + territory + mate signaling + memory

object play
→ curiosity + manipulation + problem solving

group play
→ trust + role negotiation + boundary learning

Then abstract the useful pattern away from the species:

animal behavior
→ latent function
→ play structure
→ human-safe analogue
→ test whether it produces the same kind of engagement

So instead of “copy the animal,” you borrow the game mechanic.

A raven’s play might inspire cooperative object games. A bowerbird’s display could inspire creative courtship rituals or design competitions. Dolphin chase/play could become timing and coordination games. Canid play bows are interesting as a kind of meta-signal: “what follows is play, not aggression.” That alone is a useful human design primitive.

I’d call the operator:

Ethological Game Mining

observe behavior
→ infer likely function cautiously
→ extract interaction pattern
→ remove species-specific substrate
→ reinstantiate as human play

And yes, the mating layer is especially rich because courtship often already bundles:

signal
risk
beauty
performance
choice
reciprocity
timing
competition
consent-like approach/withdrawal dynamics

So there is a huge library there for designing playful, voluntary interaction without needing to invent everything from scratch.